@meta
id: 24-04
title: Drill — one slot up
chapter: 24
type: drill
skills: ex-move
par: 13
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: Alt-Up
open: registry.ts

@brief
Eight times: the `CustomerRepository` registration on line 4 belongs
above `CustomerProvider` on line 3, because a provider must not be
registered before its repository. Go to line 4, bubble it up, save.

`:m-2` and nothing else. By the end it should feel like one key.

@keys
| `4G` | to line 4 |
| `:m-2` | the current line, one slot up |

@hints
Cursor on the line, then `:m-2`. The `-2` is the line the text lands
after: two above you, so you end up one above where you were.
--
`4G:m-2` and Enter, `:wq`.

@solution
    4G :m-2<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Eleven keystrokes. The alternatives are `ddkP`, which goes through the
unnamed register and clobbers whatever you had in it, and `:4m2`, which
needs you to read two line numbers. `:m-2` needs neither, which is why
it is the one that survives contact with a real file.

@start registry.ts
container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('OrderResolver', { useClass: OrderResolver });

@expected registry.ts
container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('OrderResolver', { useClass: OrderResolver });
